course: biology
lesson: 350
title: Farm animals by numbers: stomachs, hooves, toes, eggs and litres
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A farm is a great place to count, and every number tells a story. A cow stands on two big toes on each foot, with two little dewclaws higher up, while a horse runs on just one toe per foot, its hoof a giant toenail.
explain: Let a cow lick your hand and you will see she has no top front teeth at all, only a hard pad of gum: she wraps her tongue round the grass, pulls it against the pad and tears it. A cow's stomach has four chambers, an alpaca's three.
explain: Count teats and you can guess family sizes: a ewe has two teats and twins are most common, while a sow has about fourteen for her litter of eight to twelve piglets, and each piglet keeps its own favourite teat.
explain: Count toes on a hen and you get four, three forward and one back, unless she is a fluffy Silkie with five. Numbers like these let you check cartoons, where animals often have the wrong number of everything.
fact: Cows have 32 teeth but none at the top front, only a hard pad of gum, and sheep and goats have the same pad.
fact: Cows, sheep, goats and pigs walk on two toes, called cloven hooves, with two smaller dewclaws higher up at the back, while a horse walks on one toe on each foot.
fact: A sow usually has about 8 to 12 piglets and around 14 teats, each piglet goes back to its own favourite teat, and a pig has four toes on each foot but walks on the middle two.
fact: A dairy goat gives about two to four litres of milk a day; goats do not eat tin cans, they just nibble the labels, and their rectangular pupils help them watch all around for danger.
fact: Duck eggs are larger with thicker shells and bigger yolks, some breeds like the Khaki Campbell lay nearly 300 a year, and ducks lay early in the morning, so they are kept in until then.
fact: A ewe has two teats, which is why twins are most common, and a sheep's fleece weighs a few kilograms each year.
fact: A study found sheep can recognise up to about 50 sheep faces and remember them for years.
fact: An adult horse has about 36 to 44 teeth that keep growing out slowly as they wear down, with a gap where the bit of a bridle sits, and vets can guess its age from their shape and wear.
fact: Most hens have four toes, three forward and one back to grip a perch, Silkies have five, and cockerels grow a sharp spur on the back of each leg.
fact: Alpacas and llamas have two toes on each foot with soft pads that do not damage the ground, their stomachs have three chambers while a cow's has four, and alpacas share one toilet pile in a field.
fact: A cow can live about 20 years, a horse about 25 to 30, a sheep about 10 to 12, a hen about 8 and a goat about 15, but most farm animals are sold or slaughtered long before they are old.
fact: A horse named Old Billy was reported to have lived to 62 in England in the 1800s.
exercise: What does a cow have instead of top front teeth? | A hard pad of gum | A row of sharp fangs | A beak like a bird | Nothing at all, just a gap
exercise: How many teeth does a cow have? | 32 | 12 | 100 | 4
exercise: How many toes does a cow walk on on each foot? | Two | Just one | Three | All five
exercise: What are the two little toes higher up on a cow's foot called? | Dewclaws | Spurs | Hooflets | Fetlocks
exercise: How many toes does a horse walk on on each foot? | One | Two, like a cow | Three | Four
exercise: About how many teats does a sow have? | About 14 | Only about 2 | Only about 4 | About 40 or more
exercise: Why do piglets each keep their own teat? | So they do not have to fight every time they feed | The mother pig gives each teat a name and a piglet | Each teat gives a different flavour | They are tied to it by the farmer
exercise: About how much milk does a dairy goat give in a day? | About two to four litres | About 30 litres | About a teaspoon | About 100 litres
exercise: Do goats really eat tin cans? | No, they only nibble the paper labels | Yes, their stomachs can break down the metal | Yes, but only empty ones | Only baby goats do
exercise: What shape are a goat's pupils? | Rectangular | Round | Star-shaped | Heart-shaped
exercise: When do ducks usually lay their eggs? | Early in the morning | At midnight | At lunchtime | Only in the evening
exercise: How many teats does a ewe have? | Two | Four | About six | Fourteen
exercise: About how many sheep faces can a sheep recognise, a study found? | About 50 | About 2 | None at all | About 5000
exercise: How can a vet guess a horse's age? | From the shape and wear of its teeth | From how grey the hairs of its tail have gone | By counting its hooves | From how loudly it neighs
exercise: How many toes does a Silkie hen have on each foot? | Five | Four, like a duck | Three | Only two
exercise: How many chambers does an alpaca's stomach have? | Three | Four | One | Seven
exercise: Which farm animal usually lives longest? | The horse | The farmyard hen | The woolly sheep | The nanny goat
test: In the film Barnyard, the male cows have big udders and walk on their back legs. What do the numbers say? | Only cows that have calved make milk, and real cattle walk on four feet with two toes each | Male cows have four udders each and give more milk than the females do | Cattle walk on two toes in total | Nothing is wrong
test: In a Tom and Jerry style cartoon, a cow waves hooves with five fingers each. Count it right: how many toes does a real cow stand on altogether? | 8 | 4 | 20 | 16
test: Count them: 3 hens and 1 Silkie scratch in the yard. How many toes are there altogether? | 34 | 32 | 16 | 40
test: Count them: a farmer has 2 horses and 3 cows. How many tails, and how many toes do they stand on in total? | 5 tails and 32 toes | 5 tails and 20 toes | 10 tails and 32 toes | 5 tails and 40 toes
test: «sheep's had triplets, she'll be fine, she's a sheep» Why might triplets be harder for a ewe? | She has only two teats, so one lamb must wait or be bottle-fed | She has six teats, which is far too many for her to manage at once | Triplets are always too big to stand | Ewes do not feed their lambs
test: «my goat ate a whole can of beans, tin and all» What really happened? | It nibbled the paper label, not the tin | Goats can digest metal | It swallowed the tin whole and was fine | Goats only eat tins, not grass
test: How much milk would 5 goats giving 3 litres a day each give in a week? | 105 litres | 15 litres | 35 litres | 21 litres
test: «sheep are thick, can't tell one face from another» What did a study find? | Sheep can recognise about 50 sheep faces and remember them for years | Sheep cannot see faces at all, only the colour of the wool | Sheep remember faces for one minute | Sheep recognise only their own reflection
test: Why would you keep laying ducks in their house until mid-morning? | They lay early in the morning, so the eggs end up in the house, not lost in the field | Ducks are afraid of the morning sun | Ducks sleep until noon | The eggs hatch if laid outside
test: «horse's teeth just wear away to nothing, poor thing» Why does that not usually happen? | Horses' teeth keep growing out slowly as they wear down | Horses lose all their teeth and grow a whole new set every year | Horses do not chew their food | Horses' teeth are made of metal
test: Count them: a sow has 11 piglets and 14 teats. If each piglet keeps one teat, how many teats are left free? | 3 | 11 | 25 | 0
test: A boy wants a farm animal that could be his friend the longest. Which should he choose? | A horse, which can live about 25 to 30 years | A hen, which can live about 50 years | A sheep, which can live about 40 years | A goat, which can live about 100 years
exam: Identify the means by which a cow, lacking upper incisors, severs grass. | The tongue draws the grass against a hard dental pad, whereupon it is torn | The grass is severed by sharp upper fangs | The grass is cut by the hooves prior to consumption | The grass is swallowed whole without being severed
exam: State the number of toes upon which a horse bears its weight on each foot. | One | Two | Three | Four
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the number of teats and the size of the litter in farm animals? | The ewe possesses two teats and twins are most common, whereas the sow possesses approximately fourteen teats for a litter of eight to twelve | The ewe possesses fourteen teats and the sow two | All farm animals possess four teats irrespective of litter size | The number of teats bears no relation whatsoever to the offspring
exam: Compute the aggregate number of stomach chambers possessed by two cows and one alpaca. | Eleven | Nine | Ten | Twelve
